body: "# Semgrep finding import\n\nSemgrep finding id: 874840455\nRule: javascript.lang.security.audit.prototype-pollution.prototype-pollution-loop.prototype-pollution-loop\nProduct: Code\nSeverity: Medium\nStatus: Open\nConfidence: Low\nCategory: security\nIs Pro Rule: False\nSemgrep autotriage result: False positive\nSemgrep file risk tag: Developer Scripts\nRepository: unbraind/pm-cli\nBranch: refs/heads/main\nCreated at: 2026-07-03 12:04:25.346273\nLast opened at: 2026-07-03 12:04:25.327913\n\nLinks:\n- Code: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/6f511706628f18743a00a1cfe851e8d1f492684e/src/cli/commands/test.ts#L1383\n- Semgrep: https://semgrep.dev/orgs/unbrained/findings/874840455\n- Repository: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli\n\nRule description:\nPossibility of prototype polluting function detected. By adding or modifying attributes of an object prototype, it is possible to create attributes that exist on every object, or replace critical attributes with malicious ones. This can be problematic if the software depends on existence or non-existence of certain attributes, or uses pre-defined attributes of object prototype (such as hasOwnProperty, toString or valueOf). Possible mitigations might be: freezing the object prototype, using an object without prototypes (via Object.create(null) ), blocking modifications of attributes that resolve to object prototype, using Map instead of object.\n\nAutotriage reason:\nThis code is reading a path through an existing object, not writing attacker-controlled properties onto an object or prototype, so it does not create prototype pollution. The low-confidence rule matched a generic dynamic property access pattern inside a loop, but in this context it is a read-only JSON path lookup and not a meaningful security issue by itself.\n\nImported from downloaded CSV:\nSemgrep_Code_Combined_Findings_2026_07_06.csv (local download, not committed)"
